# Defaults and restrictions

Defaults describe the opening crop. Restrictions constrain both that opening state and later user or programmatic changes. Configure the built-in settings as flat props on `Cropper`.

## Opening coordinates

`defaultCoordinates` accepts an object or a function of state and settings. It is applied when an image loads or you call `reset()`.

<Example source={DefaultCoordinatesExampleSource} title="DefaultCoordinatesExample">
	<DefaultCoordinatesExample />
</Example>

If you only need a size or position, use `defaultSize` or `defaultPosition`. The remaining part is computed by the core. This example uses a default-size function to fill the available image region.

<Example source={FillingCropperExampleSource} title="FillingCropperExample">
	<FillingCropperExample />
</Example>

## Opening visible area and priority

The visible area describes the viewport in image coordinates, including pan and zoom. Use `defaultVisibleArea` to restore it. `Priority.coordinates` computes the crop first; `Priority.visibleArea` computes the view first and fits the crop to it.

<Example source={DefaultVisibleAreaSource} title="DefaultVisibleArea">
	<DefaultVisibleArea />
</Example>

Defaults run on reset. A layout refresh preserves the current view where restrictions permit. To reapply a view after resizing without discarding rotation or the user's crop, see [layout and lifecycle](/docs/guides/layout-and-lifecycle#keep-the-opening-view).

## Image restrictions

`ImageRestriction` is exported from the package root:

| Value | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `fitArea` | Keep the image within the cropper area as far as possible; the default. |
| `fillArea` | Keep the cropper area covered by the image. |
| `stencil` | Keep the selection covered by the image. |
| `none` | Remove these image-position and scale restrictions. |

<Example source={ImageRestrictionExampleSource} title="ImageRestrictionExample">
	<ImageRestrictionExample />
</Example>

## Size restrictions

`minWidth`, `minHeight`, `maxWidth` and `maxHeight` use image pixels. A custom `sizeRestrictions` function can interpret them differently. This example uses percentages and the transformed image size.

<Example source={CustomRestrictionsExampleSource} title="CustomRestrictionsExample">
	<CustomRestrictionsExample />
</Example>

The functions and types used by this demo are re-exported from `svelte-advanced-cropper`. For additional application settings shared by algorithms, use the typed `settings` extension object; do not put built-in keys in it. See [core settings](/docs/core/settings).

## Fixed stencil size

`FixedCropper` takes a required `stencilSize`, either a size or a function of state and settings. It controls the displayed frame size, rather than the exported pixel dimensions. The fixed-stencil strategy combines it with the registered stencil ratio and fits it within the boundary.

<Example source={FixedSizeExampleSource} title="FixedSizeExample">
	<FixedSizeExample />
</Example>

## Adjust the stencil while zooming

`transformImage={{ adjustStencil: true }}` lets ordinary `Cropper` adjust the selection during image transforms. Toggle it below and compare zoom behaviour near size limits. `FixedCropper` forces it to `false` to preserve the fixed frame.

<Example source={AdjustStencilExampleSource} title="AdjustStencilExample">
	<AdjustStencilExample />
</Example>

## Default transforms

`defaultTransforms` supplies opening rotation and flips. Like other defaults, it takes effect on reset. This demo explicitly resets after changing its controls.

<Example source={DefaultTransformsExampleSource} title="DefaultTransformsExample">
	<DefaultTransformsExample />
</Example>

A cropper-level aspect ratio normally takes precedence over a stencil suggestion. A custom `stencilConstraints` strategy can change that precedence; see [stencil context](/docs/reference/stencil-context).
